'''vague†''' (vāg), ''v. i.'' [Sc. also ''vaig''; < F. ''vaguer'', wander, = Sp. Pg. ''vagar'', ''vaguear'' = It. ''vagare'', < L. ''vagari'', wander, < ''vagus'', wandering: see ''vague'', ''a.'' Cf. ''vagary'', ''v.''] To wander; rove; roam; play the vagrant.
